arXiv:2510.06780v1 [cs.CL] 08 Oct 2025</div>
</div><div class="ltx_page_content">
<article class="ltx_document ltx_authors_1line">
<h1 class="ltx_title ltx_title_document">Foundations of LLM Knowledge Materialization:
<br class="ltx_break">Termination, Reproducibility, Robustness</h1>
<div class="ltx_authors">
<span class="ltx_creator ltx_role_author">
<span class="ltx_personname">Luca Giordano
</span></span>
<span class="ltx_author_before">  </span><span class="ltx_creator ltx_role_author">
<span class="ltx_personname">Simon Razniewski
</span><span class="ltx_author_notes"><span class="ltx_author_notes_content">
<span class="ltx_contact ltx_role_affiliation"><span class="ltx_contact_name">Affiliation: </span>ScaDS.AI Dresden/Leipzig &amp; TU Dresden, Germany
</span>
<span class="ltx_contact ltx_role_email"><span class="ltx_contact_name">Email: </span><a href="mailto:"><span id="id1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_typewriter">{luca.giordano,simon.razniewski}@tu-dresden.de</span></a>
</span></span></span></span></div>

<div id="abstract1" class="ltx_abstract"><h6 class="ltx_title ltx_title_abstract">Abstract</h6>
    
<p id="abstract1.1" class="ltx_p">Large Language Models (LLMs) encode substantial factual knowledge, yet measuring and systematizing this knowledge remains challenging. Converting it into structured format—for example through recursive extraction approaches such as the GPTKB methodology <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_cite"><a href="#bib.bib10" title="" class="ltx_ref">Hu et al. (2025b)</a></cite>—is still underexplored. Key open questions include whether such extraction can terminate, whether its outputs are reproducible, and how robust they are to variations.</p>
    
<p id="abstract1.2" class="ltx_p">We systematically study LLM knowledge materialization using <span id="abstract1.2.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_italic">miniGPTKBs</span> (domain-specific, tractable subcrawls), analyzing termination, reproducibility, and robustness across three categories of metrics: yield, lexical similarity, and semantic similarity. We experiment with four variations (seed, language, randomness, model) and three illustrative domains (from history, entertainment, and finance).</p>
    
<p id="abstract1.3" class="ltx_p">Our findings show (i) high termination rates, though model-dependent; (ii) mixed reproducibility; and (iii) robustness that varies by perturbation type—high for seeds and temperature, lower for languages and models. These results suggest that LLM knowledge materialization can reliably surface core knowledge, while also revealing important limitations.</p>
  
</div>
<div id="p1" class="ltx_para ltx_align_bottom">
<p id="p1.1" class="ltx_p ltx_align_center" style="width:345.0pt;"><span id="p1.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_inline-block" style="width:0.0pt;"></span></p>
</div>
<section id="S1" class="ltx_section">
<h2 class="ltx_title ltx_title_section"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_section">1 </span>Introduction</h2>

<div id="S1.p1" class="ltx_para">
<blockquote id="S1.p1.1" class="ltx_quote">
<p id="S1.p1.1.1" class="ltx_p"><span id="S1.p1.1.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_italic">This was the man to whom all things were known; this was the king who knew the countries of the world.</span></p>
<p id="S1.p1.1.2" class="ltx_p"><span id="S1.p1.1.2.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_italic">— The Epic of Gilgamesh, <span id="S1.p1.1.2.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_smallcaps">Prologue</span></span></p>
</blockquote>
</div>
<figure id="S1.F1" class="ltx_figure"><img src="2510.06780v1/teaser.png" id="S1.F1.g1" class="ltx_graphics ltx_centering ltx_img_landscape" style="aspect-ratio:500/345;" width="500" height="345" alt="Refer to caption">
<figcaption class="ltx_caption ltx_centering"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_figure">Figure 1: </span>Overview of our approach.</figcaption>
</figure>
<section id="S1.SS0.SSS0.Px1" class="ltx_paragraph">
<h4 class="ltx_title ltx_title_paragraph">Motivation</h4>

<div id="S1.SS0.SSS0.Px1.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S1.SS0.SSS0.Px1.p1.1" class="ltx_p">Large Language Models (LLMs) have revitalized Artificial Intelligence (AI) and Natural Language Processing (NLP), and impacted many other domains both inside Computer Science <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_citep">(<a href="#bib.bib22" title="" class="ltx_ref">Pramanick et al., 2024</a>)</cite> and beyond, e.g. in education, healthcare, and finance <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_citep">(<a href="#bib.bib3" title="" class="ltx_ref">Bharathi Mohan et al., 2024</a>)</cite>. During pre-training, LLMs encode in their parameters vast amounts of factual knowledge <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_citep">(<a href="#bib.bib4" title="" class="ltx_ref">Bubeck et al., 2023</a>)</cite>, which can then be implicitly leveraged for a wide range of downstream tasks. However, they also exhibit shortcomings, including lack of coherence, biases, hallucinations, and factual errors <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_citep">(<a href="#bib.bib8" title="" class="ltx_ref">Holtzman et al., 2020</a>; <a href="#bib.bib11" title="" class="ltx_ref">Ji et al., 2023</a>; <a href="#bib.bib2" title="" class="ltx_ref">Berglund et al., 2024</a>)</cite>. Moreover, this factual knowledge is difficult to measure, examine, interpret, and ultimately retrieve. On-demand prompting remains the standard but imperfect means of accessing it. As <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_citet"><a href="#bib.bib5" title="" class="ltx_ref">Cohen et al. (2023)</a></cite> note, it is desirable to obtain interpretable, transparent representations of this knowledge and interact with it through tools such as query languages.</p>
</div>
<div id="S1.SS0.SSS0.Px1.p2" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S1.SS0.SSS0.Px1.p2.1" class="ltx_p">Benchmark-based “knowledge probing” <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_cite"><a href="#bib.bib21" title="" class="ltx_ref">Petroni et al. (2019)</a></cite> relies on pre-designed cloze or Q&amp;A tasks, which, as pointed out by <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_citet"><a href="#bib.bib10" title="" class="ltx_ref">Hu et al. (2025b)</a></cite>, suffers from availability bias <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_citep">(<a href="#bib.bib27" title="" class="ltx_ref">Tversky and Kahneman, 1973</a>)</cite>, i.e. it cannot reveal knowledge outside the benchmark’s scope.</p>
</div>
<div id="S1.SS0.SSS0.Px1.p3" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S1.SS0.SSS0.Px1.p3.1" class="ltx_p">A more promising approach was proposed by <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_citet"><a href="#bib.bib5" title="" class="ltx_ref">Cohen et al. (2023)</a></cite> and popularized by <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_citet"><a href="#bib.bib10" title="" class="ltx_ref">Hu et al. (2025b)</a>; <a href="#bib.bib9" title="" class="ltx_ref">Hu et al. (2025a)</a></cite>, termed “knowledge crawling”, i.e. prompt-based, recursive, and open-ended knowledge extraction that stores results in a structured knowledge base (KB) of entities and relations, linked in statements in the form of s-p-o-triples. KBs are human-readable, interpretable, interconnected, comparable and easily queried or updated, making them a natural choice for explicit knowledge representation <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_citep">(<a href="#bib.bib5" title="" class="ltx_ref">Cohen et al., 2023</a>)</cite>.</p>
</div>
<div id="S1.SS0.SSS0.Px1.p4" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S1.SS0.SSS0.Px1.p4.1" class="ltx_p">The GPTKB approach at LLM knowledge materialization by <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_citet"><a href="#bib.bib10" title="" class="ltx_ref">Hu et al. (2025b)</a>; <a href="#bib.bib9" title="" class="ltx_ref">Hu et al. (2025a)</a></cite> represents a prototypical execution at impressive scale, but is still arbitrarily stopped at 100 M triples, and fundamental methodological questions remain unanswered. The present work asks whether the GPTKB approach captures the essence of an LLM’s factual knowledge.</p>
</div>
<div id="S1.SS0.SSS0.Px1.p5" class="ltx_para ltx_noindent"><span class="ltx_inline-block"><svg id="S1.SS0.SSS0.Px1.p5.pic1" class="ltx_picture" height="203.99" overflow="visible" version="1.1" viewBox="0 0 653.15 203.99" width="653.15"><g style="--ltx-stroke-color:#000000;--ltx-fill-color:#000000;" fill="#000000" stroke="#000000" stroke-width="0.4pt" transform="translate(0,203.99) matrix(1 0 0 -1 0 0)"><g style="--ltx-fill-color:#30BFBF;" fill="#30BFBF" fill-opacity="1.0"><path style="stroke:none" d="M 0 5.91 L 0 198.09 C 0 201.35 2.64 203.99 5.91 203.99 L 647.24 203.99 C 650.51 203.99 653.15 201.35 653.15 198.09 L 653.15 5.91 C 653.15 2.64 650.51 0 647.24 0 L 5.91 0 C 2.64 0 0 2.64 0 5.91 Z"></path></g><g style="--ltx-fill-color:#F2FFFF;" fill="#F2FFFF" fill-opacity="1.0"><path style="stroke:none" d="M 1.97 5.91 L 1.97 179.88 L 651.18 179.88 L 651.18 5.91 C 651.18 3.73 649.42 1.97 647.24 1.97 L 5.91 1.97 C 3.73 1.97 1.97 3.73 1.97 5.91 Z"></path></g><g fill-opacity="1.0" transform="matrix(1.0 0.0 0.0 1.0 10.06 188.48)"><foreignObject style="--ltx-fo-width:39.78em;--ltx-fo-height:0.69em;--ltx-fo-depth:0.19em;font-size:10pt;" height="12.3" overflow="visible" transform="matrix(1 0 0 -1 0 9.61)" width="550.44"><span class="ltx_foreignobject_container"><span class="ltx_foreignobject_content">
<span id="S1.SS0.SSS0.Px1.p5.pic1.1" class="ltx_inline-block ltx_minipage ltx_align_bottom" style="width:39.78em;">
<span id="S1.SS0.SSS0.Px1.p5.pic1.1.1" class="ltx_p"><span id="S1.SS0.SSS0.Px1.p5.pic1.1.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold" style="--ltx-fg-color:#FFFFFF;">Research Questions</span></span>
</span></span></span></foreignObject></g><g fill-opacity="1.0" transform="matrix(1.0 0.0 0.0 1.0 10.06 156.65)"><foreignObject style="--ltx-fo-width:45.75em;--ltx-fo-height:1.19em;--ltx-fo-depth:10.69em;font-size:10pt;" height="164.51" overflow="visible" transform="matrix(1 0 0 -1 0 16.53)" width="633.05"><span class="ltx_foreignobject_container"><span class="ltx_foreignobject_content">
<span id="S1.SS0.SSS0.Px1.p5.pic1.2" class="ltx_inline-block ltx_minipage ltx_align_bottom" style="width:45.75em;">
<span id="S1.I1" class="ltx_itemize" style="--ltx-enum-leftmargin:3em;">
<span id="S1.I1.ix1" class="ltx_item" style="list-style-type:none;"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_item"><span id="S1.I1.ix1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">RQ1:</span></span> 
<span id="S1.I1.ix1.p1" class="ltx_para">
<span id="S1.I1.ix1.p1.1" class="ltx_p"><span id="S1.I1.ix1.p1.1.1" class="ltx_text" style="--ltx-fg-color:#000000;">Can LLM knowledge materialization reach </span><span id="S1.I1.ix1.p1.1.2" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold" style="--ltx-fg-color:#000000;">termination</span><span id="S1.I1.ix1.p1.1.3" class="ltx_text" style="--ltx-fg-color:#000000;">, or is it prone to endless hallucination?</span></span>
</span></span>
<span id="S1.I1.ix2" class="ltx_item" style="list-style-type:none;padding-top:0.0pt;"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_item"><span id="S1.I1.ix2.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">RQ2:</span></span> 
<span id="S1.I1.ix2.p1" class="ltx_para">
<span id="S1.I1.ix2.p1.1" class="ltx_p"><span id="S1.I1.ix2.p1.1.1" class="ltx_text" style="--ltx-fg-color:#000000;">Given that LLMs are nondeterministic, how </span><span id="S1.I1.ix2.p1.1.2" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold" style="--ltx-fg-color:#000000;">reproducible</span><span id="S1.I1.ix2.p1.1.3" class="ltx_text" style="--ltx-fg-color:#000000;"> is LLM knowledge materialization?</span></span>
</span></span>
<span id="S1.I1.ix3" class="ltx_item" style="list-style-type:none;padding-top:0.0pt;"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_item"><span id="S1.I1.ix3.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">RQ3:</span></span> 
<span id="S1.I1.ix3.p1" class="ltx_para">
<span id="S1.I1.ix3.p1.1" class="ltx_p"><span id="S1.I1.ix3.p1.1.1" class="ltx_text" style="--ltx-fg-color:#000000;">To what extent are LLM knowledge materializations </span><span id="S1.I1.ix3.p1.1.2" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold" style="--ltx-fg-color:#000000;">robust</span><span id="S1.I1.ix3.p1.1.3" class="ltx_text" style="--ltx-fg-color:#000000;"> to design choices?</span></span>
</span></span>
</span>
</span></span></span></foreignObject></g></g></svg></span>
</div>
<div id="S1.SS0.SSS0.Px1.p6" class="ltx_para ltx_noindent">
<p id="S1.SS0.SSS0.Px1.p6.1" class="ltx_p"><span id="S1.SS0.SSS0.Px1.p6.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">Contributions.</span> Our core contributions are:</p>
<ol id="S1.I2" class="ltx_enumerate">
<li id="S1.I2.i1" class="ltx_item" style="list-style-type:none;"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_item">1.</span> 
<div id="S1.I2.i1.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S1.I2.i1.p1.1" class="ltx_p">We introduce the concept of <span id="S1.I2.i1.p1.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_italic">miniGPTKBs</span>, i.e. domain-specific, tractable subcrawls of the whole LLM knowledge, such as the three illustrative examples of babylonGPTKB (history), tbbtGPTKB<span id="footnote1" class="ltx_note ltx_role_footnote"><sup class="ltx_note_mark">1</sup><span class="ltx_note_outer"><span class="ltx_note_content"><sup class="ltx_note_mark">1</sup>
                    <span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_note">1</span>
                    
                    
                    
                  TBBT = The Big Bang Theory, an American TV sitcom.</span></span></span> (entertainment), and dax40GPTKB (finance);</p>
</div></li>
<li id="S1.I2.i2" class="ltx_item" style="list-style-type:none;"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_item">2.</span> 
<div id="S1.I2.i2.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S1.I2.i2.p1.1" class="ltx_p">We provide the first proof that the GPTKB approach at LLM knowledge materialization can terminate;</p>
</div></li>
<li id="S1.I2.i3" class="ltx_item" style="list-style-type:none;"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_item">3.</span> 
<div id="S1.I2.i3.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S1.I2.i3.p1.1" class="ltx_p">In terms of reproducibility, we observe mixed signals across runs, with low lexical similarity, intermediate semantic similarity, but high similarity in quantitative yield;</p>
</div></li>
<li id="S1.I2.i4" class="ltx_item" style="list-style-type:none;"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_item">4.</span> 
<div id="S1.I2.i4.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S1.I2.i4.p1.1" class="ltx_p">In terms of robustness, we find high robustness to different seed entities and temperature values, but lower robustness to different languages and models.</p>
</div></li>
</ol>
<p id="S1.SS0.SSS0.Px1.p6.2" class="ltx_p">To address the mixed stability (reproducibility &amp; robustness), we also show that ensembling can significantly enhance the stability of the output. Altogether, our results confirm that, especially with ensembling, the GPTKB methodology provides a stable view of the core of a specific LLM’s factual knowledge, with the two main sources of instability being a multilingual setting and the properties/capabilities of specific LLMs.</p>
</div>
<div id="S1.SS0.SSS0.Px1.p7" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S1.SS0.SSS0.Px1.p7.1" class="ltx_p">Data and code are available under the CC-BY 4.0 license on this work’s GitHub repository.<span id="footnote2" class="ltx_note ltx_role_footnote"><sup class="ltx_note_mark">2</sup><span class="ltx_note_outer"><span class="ltx_note_content"><sup class="ltx_note_mark">2</sup>
              <span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_note">2</span>
              
              
              
            <a href="https://anonymous.4open.science/r/miniGPTKB-0DE7/" title="" class="ltx_ref ltx_href">https://anonymous.4open.science/r/miniGPTKB-0DE7/</a></span></span></span></p>
</div>
</section>
</section>
<section id="S2" class="ltx_section">
<h2 class="ltx_title ltx_title_section"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_section">2 </span>Related Work</h2>

<section id="S2.SS0.SSS0.Px1" class="ltx_paragraph">
<h4 class="ltx_title ltx_title_paragraph">Knowledge extraction from LLMs</h4>

<div id="S2.SS0.SSS0.Px1.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S2.SS0.SSS0.Px1.p1.1" class="ltx_p"><cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_citet"><a href="#bib.bib21" title="" class="ltx_ref">Petroni et al. (2019)</a></cite> first proposed treating LMs as knowledge bases with the LAMA benchmark, using cloze-style prompts derived from <span id="S2.SS0.SSS0.Px1.p1.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_italic">(s, p, o)</span> triples, and <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_citet"><a href="#bib.bib13" title="" class="ltx_ref">Kassner et al. (2021)</a></cite> extended it to 53 languages. To address the ad-hoc nature of hand-crafted prompts, some works investigated automatic prompt optimization <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_cite"><a href="#bib.bib12" title="" class="ltx_ref">Jiang et al. (2020)</a>; <a href="#bib.bib24" title="" class="ltx_ref">Shin et al. (2020)</a>; <a href="#bib.bib30" title="" class="ltx_ref">Wu et al. (2025)</a></cite>.</p>
</div>
<div id="S2.SS0.SSS0.Px1.p2" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S2.SS0.SSS0.Px1.p2.1" class="ltx_p"><cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_citet"><a href="#bib.bib25" title="" class="ltx_ref">Swamy et al. (2021)</a></cite> analyzed knowledge acquisition during training with cloze probing, showing correlations between model knowledge and size. More recently, <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_citet"><a href="#bib.bib5" title="" class="ltx_ref">Cohen et al. (2023)</a></cite> extracted KGs from LMs via a “crawling” method starting from seed entities. Similarly, <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_citet"><a href="#bib.bib20" title="" class="ltx_ref">Parović et al. (2025)</a></cite> generated domain-specific KGs using LLM-produced schemas and Chain-of-Verification prompting.</p>
</div>
</section>
<section id="S2.SS0.SSS0.Px2" class="ltx_paragraph">
<h4 class="ltx_title ltx_title_paragraph">Reproducibility of LLM outputs</h4>

<div id="S2.SS0.SSS0.Px2.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S2.SS0.SSS0.Px2.p1.1" class="ltx_p"><cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_citet"><a href="#bib.bib32" title="" class="ltx_ref">Zheng et al. (2024)</a></cite> studied LLM reproducibility, termed ’consistency’, evaluating 26 models and finding that more factual models tend to give consistent—sometimes incorrect—responses.
<cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_citet"><a href="#bib.bib23" title="" class="ltx_ref">Rajan et al. (2024)</a></cite> introduced KonTest, an automated framework to detect inconsistencies in LLM world knowledge using semantically equivalent queries.</p>
</div>
</section>
<section id="S2.SS0.SSS0.Px3" class="ltx_paragraph">
<h4 class="ltx_title ltx_title_paragraph">Robustness of LLM outputs</h4>

<div id="S2.SS0.SSS0.Px3.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S2.SS0.SSS0.Px3.p1.1" class="ltx_p"><cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_citet"><a href="#bib.bib6" title="" class="ltx_ref">Deng et al. (2024)</a></cite> showed that ChatGPT and GPT-4 struggle with language variation, being significantly more likely to produce harmful content when prompted in low-resource languages instead of higher-resource ones.
<cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_citet"><a href="#bib.bib14" title="" class="ltx_ref">Lim et al. (2025)</a></cite> investigated the phenomenon of cross-lingual transfer in LLMs i.e. their ability to generalize knowledge from one language to another, and suggest that LLMs’ inconsistent outputs across languages may result from relying on language-specific subspaces rather than a shared multilingual semantic space.</p>
</div>
<figure id="S2.F2" class="ltx_figure"><img src="2510.06780v1/overview-method.png" id="S2.F2.g1" class="ltx_graphics ltx_centering ltx_img_landscape" style="aspect-ratio:476/175;" width="476" height="175" alt="Refer to caption">
<figcaption class="ltx_caption ltx_centering"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_figure">Figure 2: </span>Overview of research questions and methodology.</figcaption>
</figure>
</section>
</section>
<section id="S3" class="ltx_section">
<h2 class="ltx_title ltx_title_section"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_section">3 </span>GPTKB Method</h2>

<div id="S3.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S3.p1.1" class="ltx_p">The GPTKB approach at LLM knowledge materialization introduced by <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_citet"><a href="#bib.bib10" title="" class="ltx_ref">Hu et al. (2025b)</a></cite>, further refined in <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_citet"><a href="#bib.bib9" title="" class="ltx_ref">Hu et al. (2025a)</a></cite>, and inspired by <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_citet"><a href="#bib.bib5" title="" class="ltx_ref">Cohen et al. (2023)</a></cite>, is a form of LLM knowledge crawling, and can be understood as a prompt-based, recursive, and open-ended knowledge extraction process that stores results in a structured format, such as a knowledge base (KB).</p>
</div>
<div id="S3.p2" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S3.p2.1" class="ltx_p">At a high level, the workflow consists of two fundamental phases, termed Knowledge Elicitation and Knowledge Consolidation.<span id="footnote3" class="ltx_note ltx_role_footnote"><sup class="ltx_note_mark">3</sup><span class="ltx_note_outer"><span class="ltx_note_content"><sup class="ltx_note_mark">3</sup>
            <span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_note">3</span>
            
            
            
          Here we focus only on the first phase. For further details on the second phase, refer to <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_cite"><a href="#bib.bib10" title="" class="ltx_ref">Hu et al. (2025b)</a></cite>.</span></span></span>
The process starts from a single arbitrary seed entity, and the LLM is prompted to return knowledge about this subject in the form of <span id="S3.p2.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_italic">(s, p, o)</span> triples. Using the model itself for Named Entity Recognition (NER), new named entities are then iteratively identified among the objects of these triples. These objects become new subjects and the graph is further expanded recursively.</p>
</div>
<div id="S3.p3" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S3.p3.1" class="ltx_p"><cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_citet"><a href="#bib.bib10" title="" class="ltx_ref">Hu et al. (2025b)</a></cite> executed a single run with <span id="S3.p3.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">GPT-4o-mini</span>, only <span id="S3.p3.1.2" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">in English</span>, with a <span id="S3.p3.1.3" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">single arbitrary seed entity</span> (<span id="S3.p3.1.4" class="ltx_text ltx_font_italic">Vannevar Bush</span>) and the <span id="S3.p3.1.5" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">temperature set to 0</span>. The elicitation phase was artificially capped at BFS-depth 10, yielding 101M triples for 2.9M entities, 567K relations and 4,715 classes. The process took 27 hours and cost $3,500 in total. In follow-up work, over $14k were spent on a similar-sized run with GPT-4.1 <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_cite"><a href="#bib.bib9" title="" class="ltx_ref">Hu et al. (2025a)</a></cite>. Although the choice to execute a single, capped run is understandable considering the resources needed, no proof of termination could be provided, and reproducibility and robustness remain open, too. The methodology and experiments in this work aim at shedding light on these questions.</p>
</div>
</section>
<section id="S4" class="ltx_section">
<h2 class="ltx_title ltx_title_section"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_section">4 </span>Methodology</h2>

<div id="S4.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S4.p1.1" class="ltx_p">This work adopts the methodology of the GPTKB approach and expands on it to test for termination, reproducibility, and robustness of the knowledge materialization process through <span id="S4.p1.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_italic">miniGPTKBs</span>. To compare the resulting KBs of different runs under different experimental settings, we identify their main <span id="S4.p1.1.2" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">structural categories</span>: named entities, literals, predicates, classes, and triples.</p>
</div>
<div id="S4.p2" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S4.p2.1" class="ltx_p">We test across multiple <span id="S4.p2.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">topics</span> to assess validity across domains. Suitable topics for <span id="S4.p2.1.2" class="ltx_text ltx_font_italic">miniGPTKBs</span> in the context of termination and stability testing balance specificity (for tractability), heterogeneity (for varied entity types), and independence from the LLM’s cutoff date. We select three: ancient Babylon (history), The Big Bang Theory (entertainment), and the DAX 40 German stock market index (finance). All experiments use the prompts of <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_citet"><a href="#bib.bib10" title="" class="ltx_ref">Hu et al. (2025b)</a></cite>, slightly adapted to contain the output of the model within the topics. The full prompts used are found in Appendix <a href="#A1" title="Appendix A Prompts ‣ Foundations of LLM Knowledge Materialization:Termination, Reproducibility, Robustness" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">A</span></a>.</p>
</div>
<section id="S4.SS1" class="ltx_subsection">
<h3 class="ltx_title ltx_title_subsection"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_subsection">4.1 </span>Metrics</h3>

<div id="S4.SS1.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S4.SS1.p1.1" class="ltx_p">Termination is easily reported as a binary observation. To evaluate reproducibility and robustness per structural category across runs, we define three types of metrics:</p>
<ol id="S4.I1" class="ltx_enumerate">
<li id="S4.I1.i1" class="ltx_item" style="list-style-type:none;"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_item">1.</span> 
<div id="S4.I1.i1.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S4.I1.i1.p1.1" class="ltx_p"><span id="S4.I1.i1.p1.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">Yield (Y)</span>: the count of elements per structural category. Where appropriate, we report its coefficient of variation <math id="S4.I1.i1.p1.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="Y_{CV}=\frac{\sigma}{\mu}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi>Y</mi><mrow><mi>C</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>V</mi></mrow></msub><mo>=</mo><mfrac><mi>σ</mi><mi>μ</mi></mfrac></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">Y_{CV}=\frac{\sigma}{\mu}</annotation></semantics></math> to measure variability relative to the mean.</p>
</div></li>
<li id="S4.I1.i2" class="ltx_item" style="list-style-type:none;"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_item">2.</span> 
<div id="S4.I1.i2.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S4.I1.i2.p1.1" class="ltx_p"><span id="S4.I1.i2.p1.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">Lexical Similarity (LS)</span>: superficial, string-based similarity, quantified in the following metric computed pair-wise:</p>
<ol id="S4.I1.i2.I1" class="ltx_enumerate">
<li id="S4.I1.i2.I1.i1" class="ltx_item" style="list-style-type:none;"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_item">(a)</span> 
<div id="S4.I1.i2.I1.i1.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S4.I1.i2.I1.i1.p1.1" class="ltx_p">Average Jaccard similarity (Equation 1 in Appendix <a href="#A2" title="Appendix B Evaluation Metrics ‣ Foundations of LLM Knowledge Materialization:Termination, Reproducibility, Robustness" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">B</span></a>)</p>
</div></li>
</ol>
</div></li>
<li id="S4.I1.i3" class="ltx_item" style="list-style-type:none;"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_item">3.</span> 
<div id="S4.I1.i3.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S4.I1.i3.p1.1" class="ltx_p"><span id="S4.I1.i3.p1.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">Semantic Similarity (SS)</span>: deeper, embedding-based similarity, quantified in the following two metrics computed pair-wise:</p>
<ol id="S4.I1.i3.I1" class="ltx_enumerate">
<li id="S4.I1.i3.I1.i1" class="ltx_item" style="list-style-type:none;"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_item">(a)</span> 
<div id="S4.I1.i3.I1.i1.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S4.I1.i3.I1.i1.p1.1" class="ltx_p">Average Cosine-based Hausdorff similarity (Equations 2 and 3 in Appendix <a href="#A2" title="Appendix B Evaluation Metrics ‣ Foundations of LLM Knowledge Materialization:Termination, Reproducibility, Robustness" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">B</span></a>)<span id="footnote4" class="ltx_note ltx_role_footnote"><sup class="ltx_note_mark">4</sup><span class="ltx_note_outer"><span class="ltx_note_content"><sup class="ltx_note_mark">4</sup>
                          <span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_note">4</span>
                          
                          
                          
                        The embedding model used is sentence-transformers/all-MiniLM-L6-v2, while for multilingual comparisons sentence-transformers/paraphrase-multilingual-mpnet-base-v2.</span></span></span></p>
</div></li>
<li id="S4.I1.i3.I1.i2" class="ltx_item" style="list-style-type:none;"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_item">(b)</span> 
<div id="S4.I1.i3.I1.i2.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S4.I1.i3.I1.i2.p1.1" class="ltx_p">Bidirectional average percentage of semantic matches (Equations <a href="#A2.E4" title="In Appendix B Evaluation Metrics ‣ Foundations of LLM Knowledge Materialization:Termination, Reproducibility, Robustness" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">4</span></a> and <a href="#A2.E5" title="In Appendix B Evaluation Metrics ‣ Foundations of LLM Knowledge Materialization:Termination, Reproducibility, Robustness" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">5</span></a> in Appendix <a href="#A2" title="Appendix B Evaluation Metrics ‣ Foundations of LLM Knowledge Materialization:Termination, Reproducibility, Robustness" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">B</span></a>)</p>
</div></li>
</ol>
</div></li>
</ol>
<p id="S4.SS1.p1.2" class="ltx_p">For example, suppose two runs yield 2 and 3 entities: <math id="S4.SS1.p1.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="r_{1}=\{\textit{Hammurabi, Marduk Temple, Nebuchadnezzar}\}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi>r</mi><mn>1</mn></msub><mo>=</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">{</mo><mtext class="ltx_mathvariant_italic">Hammurabi, Marduk Temple, Nebuchadnezzar</mtext><mo stretchy="false">}</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">r_{1}=\{\textit{Hammurabi, Marduk Temple, Nebuchadnezzar}\}</annotation></semantics></math> and <math id="S4.SS1.p1.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="r_{2}=\{\textit{Hammurabi, Temple of Marduk}\}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi>r</mi><mn>2</mn></msub><mo>=</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">{</mo><mtext class="ltx_mathvariant_italic">Hammurabi, Temple of Marduk</mtext><mo stretchy="false">}</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">r_{2}=\{\textit{Hammurabi, Temple of Marduk}\}</annotation></semantics></math>. The yield variance is <math id="S4.SS1.p1.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\tfrac{0.5}{2.5}=0.2" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mfrac><mn>0.5</mn><mn>2.5</mn></mfrac><mo>=</mo><mn>0.2</mn></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\tfrac{0.5}{2.5}=0.2</annotation></semantics></math>. They share one exact entity, giving <math id="S4.SS1.p1.m4" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\textit{AvJSim}=0.25" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mtext class="ltx_mathvariant_italic">AvJSim</mtext><mo>=</mo><mn>0.25</mn></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\textit{AvJSim}=0.25</annotation></semantics></math>. Using entity embeddings, the average cosine similarity is 0.83. With threshold <math id="S4.SS1.p1.m5" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\tau=0.95" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>τ</mi><mo>=</mo><mn>0.95</mn></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\tau=0.95</annotation></semantics></math>, <math id="S4.SS1.p1.m6" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\frac{2}{3}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mfrac><mn>2</mn><mn>3</mn></mfrac><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\frac{2}{3}</annotation></semantics></math> of <math id="S4.SS1.p1.m7" class="ltx_Math" alttext="r_{1}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>r</mi><mn>1</mn></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">r_{1}</annotation></semantics></math> and both of <math id="S4.SS1.p1.m8" class="ltx_Math" alttext="r_{2}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>r</mi><mn>2</mn></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">r_{2}</annotation></semantics></math> match, for an average match of 83.3%.</p>
</div>
<div id="S4.SS1.p2" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S4.SS1.p2.1" class="ltx_p">Additionally, we want to investigate the influence of entity popularity on stability. Here we define popularity simply as the entity’s count of statements on Wikidata <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_cite"><a href="#bib.bib28" title="" class="ltx_ref">Vrandečić and Krötzsch (2014)</a></cite>. We partition the named entities of each run in 5 popularity buckets: 4 for the quartiles of the entity list sorted by popularity, and one containing the entities not found on Wikidata. We then compute the metrics pair-wise separately for each bucket within each setting against the full sets of entities of the other runs in turn.</p>
</div>
</section>
<section id="S4.SS2" class="ltx_subsection">
<h3 class="ltx_title ltx_title_subsection"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_subsection">4.2 </span>Reproducibility Analysis</h3>

<div id="S4.SS2.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S4.SS2.p1.1" class="ltx_p">To test for reproducibility, we define a <span id="S4.SS2.p1.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">base setting</span>, and execute 30 runs divided by the three topics without altering any variable. Mirroring <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_citet"><a href="#bib.bib10" title="" class="ltx_ref">Hu et al. (2025b)</a></cite>, we define the base setting’s parameters as:</p>
<ul id="S4.I2" class="ltx_itemize">
<li id="S4.I2.i1" class="ltx_item" style="list-style-type:none;"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_item">•</span> 
<div id="S4.I2.i1.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S4.I2.i1.p1.1" class="ltx_p">Seed entity:</p>
<ul id="S4.I2.i1.I2" class="ltx_itemize">
<li id="S4.I2.i1.I2.i1" class="ltx_item" style="list-style-type:none;"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_item"><span id="S4.I2.i1.I2.i1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">–</span></span> 
<div id="S4.I2.i1.I2.i1.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S4.I2.i1.I2.i1.p1.1" class="ltx_p">babylonGPTKB : <span id="S4.I2.i1.I2.i1.p1.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_italic">Hammurabi</span></p>
</div></li>
<li id="S4.I2.i1.I2.i2" class="ltx_item" style="list-style-type:none;"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_item"><span id="S4.I2.i1.I2.i2.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">–</span></span> 
<div id="S4.I2.i1.I2.i2.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S4.I2.i1.I2.i2.p1.1" class="ltx_p">tbbtGPTKB : <span id="S4.I2.i1.I2.i2.p1.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_italic">Sheldon Cooper</span></p>
</div></li>
<li id="S4.I2.i1.I2.i3" class="ltx_item" style="list-style-type:none;"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_item"><span id="S4.I2.i1.I2.i3.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">–</span></span> 
<div id="S4.I2.i1.I2.i3.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S4.I2.i1.I2.i3.p1.1" class="ltx_p">dax40GPTKB : <span id="S4.I2.i1.I2.i3.p1.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_italic">DAX 40</span></p>
</div></li>
</ul>
</div></li>
<li id="S4.I2.i2" class="ltx_item" style="list-style-type:none;"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_item">•</span> 
<div id="S4.I2.i2.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S4.I2.i2.p1.1" class="ltx_p">Prompt language : English</p>
</div></li>
<li id="S4.I2.i3" class="ltx_item" style="list-style-type:none;"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_item">•</span> 
<div id="S4.I2.i3.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S4.I2.i3.p1.1" class="ltx_p">Model randomness (temperature) : 0.0</p>
</div></li>
<li id="S4.I2.i4" class="ltx_item" style="list-style-type:none;"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_item">•</span> 
<div id="S4.I2.i4.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S4.I2.i4.p1.1" class="ltx_p">Model : GPT-4.1-mini</p>
</div></li>
</ul>
</div>
</section>
<section id="S4.SS3" class="ltx_subsection">
<h3 class="ltx_title ltx_title_subsection"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_subsection">4.3 </span>Robustness Analysis</h3>

<div id="S4.SS3.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S4.SS3.p1.1" class="ltx_p">In this experiment we focus on the topic of Ancient Babylon. We evaluate robustness across <span id="S4.SS3.p1.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">four dimensions</span>,
executing 10 runs with different seeds, 10 runs using different languages, 10 runs with randomness introduced (temperature), and 12 runs across 6 LLMs, two for each:</p>
<ul id="S4.I3" class="ltx_itemize">
<li id="S4.I3.i1" class="ltx_item" style="list-style-type:none;"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_item">•</span> 
<div id="S4.I3.i1.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S4.I3.i1.p1.1" class="ltx_p">Seed entity : [<span id="S4.I3.i1.p1.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_italic">Hammurabi, Tashmetum, Belshazzar, Kudurru, Akitu, Hanging Gardens of Babylon, Shar-Kali-Sharri, Akkadian language, Enuma Elish, Tamkaru</span>]</p>
</div></li>
<li id="S4.I3.i2" class="ltx_item" style="list-style-type:none;"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_item">•</span> 
<div id="S4.I3.i2.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S4.I3.i2.p1.1" class="ltx_p">Prompt language : [English, Italian, German, Russian, Spanish, French, Turkish, Swedish, Arabic, Polish]</p>
</div></li>
<li id="S4.I3.i3" class="ltx_item" style="list-style-type:none;"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_item">•</span> 
<div id="S4.I3.i3.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S4.I3.i3.p1.1" class="ltx_p">Model randomness (temperature) : 1.0</p>
</div></li>
<li id="S4.I3.i4" class="ltx_item" style="list-style-type:none;"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_item">•</span> 
<div id="S4.I3.i4.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S4.I3.i4.p1.1" class="ltx_p">Model : [GPT-4.1-mini, gpt-oss-120b, Llama 4 Scout, Llama 3.3 70b, DeepSeek-R1, Teuken 7b Instruct]</p>
</div></li>
</ul>
<p id="S4.SS3.p1.2" class="ltx_p">We select a varied set of seed entities, ranging from popular kings, concepts, and locations to niche deities and objects. Regarding the language selection, we select languages from the Germanic, Romance, Slavic, Turkic and Semitic language families. We also consider a variation where we machine-translate other output to English.</p>
</div>
<div id="S4.SS3.p2" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S4.SS3.p2.1" class="ltx_p">For dimension 4, we compare 6 different LLMs: GPT-4.1-mini <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_cite"><a href="#bib.bib18" title="" class="ltx_ref">OpenAI (2025b)</a></cite>, gpt-oss-120b <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_cite"><a href="#bib.bib17" title="" class="ltx_ref">OpenAI (2025a)</a></cite>, Llama 4 Scout <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_cite"><a href="#bib.bib16" title="" class="ltx_ref">Meta (2025)</a></cite>, Llama 3.3 70b <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_cite"><a href="#bib.bib15" title="" class="ltx_ref">Meta (2024)</a></cite>, DeepSeek-R1 <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_cite"><a href="#bib.bib7" title="" class="ltx_ref">Guo et al. (2025)</a></cite>, and Teuken 7b Instruct <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_cite"><a href="#bib.bib1" title="" class="ltx_ref">Ali et al. (2024)</a></cite>. The hardware specifications used for running the local models can be found in Appendix <a href="#A5" title="Appendix E Hardware specifications for running local models ‣ Foundations of LLM Knowledge Materialization:Termination, Reproducibility, Robustness" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">E</span></a>.</p>
</div>
</section>
</section>
<section id="S5" class="ltx_section">
<h2 class="ltx_title ltx_title_section"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_section">5 </span>Results and Discussion</h2>

<div id="S5.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S5.p1.1" class="ltx_p">We report results for termination, reproducibility, and robustness across all runs and settings. All experiments were conducted between June and July 2025, and the total cost for OpenAI’s BatchAPI calls including trial runs amounts to <math id="S5.p1.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\sim" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mo>∼</mo><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\sim</annotation></semantics></math>80$.</p>
</div>
<section id="S5.SS1" class="ltx_subsection">
<h3 class="ltx_title ltx_title_subsection"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_subsection">5.1 </span>Termination</h3>

